ctap2pin — provenance note
==========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`dtls`/`falcon` modules, which are likewise self-contained crypto modules
not folded into the root NOTICE).

Provenance
----------

ctap2pin — clean-room implementation from the FIDO Alliance "Client to
Authenticator Protocol (CTAP) 2.1" specification, §6.5.6 "PIN/UV Auth
Protocol Abstract Definition", §6.5.7 "PIN/UV Auth Protocol One", and
§6.5.8 "PIN/UV Auth Protocol Two": ECDH-P256 key agreement (Z = the
big-endian x-coordinate of the shared point), the protocol-specific KDFs
(SHA-256(Z); HKDF-SHA-256 with a 32-zero-byte salt and the
"CTAP2 HMAC key" / "CTAP2 AES key" info strings), AES-256-CBC encryption
(zero IV / random `IV || ciphertext` framing), and truncated/full
HMAC-SHA-256 authentication. The CTAP 2.1 spec is a public specification —
its algorithms are not copyrightable expression (merger doctrine; see
CONVENTIONS.md §5). No reference implementation's source (libfido2,
python-fido2, or any other) was ported or consulted.

The known-answer vectors in `kat_vectors.zig` are published specification
artifacts, transcribed from their primary sources (not from any
implementation's test suite):

  - NIST SP 800-38A, Appendix F.2.5/F.2.6 (CBC-AES256 encrypt/decrypt) —
    a U.S. Government work, public domain.
  - RFC 5869, Appendix A.1 (HKDF-SHA-256) — IETF Trust, code components
    usable under the Simplified BSD terms of the Trust Legal Provisions.
  - RFC 5903, Section 8.1 (P-256 ECDH, fetched directly from
    rfc-editor.org for this pass) — same IETF Trust terms.
  - RFC 4231, Section 4.3, Test Case 2 (HMAC-SHA-256) — same IETF Trust
    terms.

`std.crypto` primitives are used via their public APIs (MIT, part of Zig
itself): `core.aes.Aes256` (the raw block cipher — CBC mode is NOT in std
and is this module's own, see `root.zig`'s module doc), `ecc.P256`,
`kdf.hkdf.HkdfSha256`, `auth.hmac.sha2.HmacSha256`, `hash.sha2.Sha256`,
and `timing_safe.eql`.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

Consumer note: this is the PIN/UV protection layer of FIDO2 / WebAuthn
passkey plumbing — both a CTAP2 platform (client) and a CTAP2
authenticator implementation sit directly on top of it (`clientPin` /
`getPinUvAuthTokenUsingPinWithPermissions` etc. are CBOR command framing
around exactly these five operations).

Oracle note (`pin_protocol_oracle_vectors.zig` / `pin_protocol_oracle_test.zig`):
the CTAP2 PIN protocol FRAMING this module composes on top of its KAT'd
primitives (encapsulate, pinHashEnc, the pinUvAuthToken exchange,
pinUvAuthParam) had no official KAT table and was previously anchored only
by round-trips against itself. It is now additionally anchored against a
single live run of Yubico's `python-fido2` SDK (`fido2.ctap2.pin`,
v2.2.1) exercised purely as a black-box test oracle — no source consulted
or copied, no design referenced (root `NOTICE` §0's black-box-oracle
exemption applies; this SDK usage needs no entry there). The captured
bytes are frozen offline in `pin_protocol_oracle_vectors.zig`; the test
suite opens no socket and runs no python at test time.

**Status: complete — both protocols implemented and validated (37/37
tests, Debug + ReleaseFast): AES-256-CBC byte-exact vs NIST SP 800-38A
F.2.5/F.2.6, HKDF vs RFC 5869 A.1, ECDH vs RFC 5903 §8.1, HMAC vs
RFC 4231, two-sided encapsulation agreement, encrypt/decrypt round-trips,
and tamper/length rejection for both protocols, PLUS the protocol
FRAMING (encapsulate → kdf → pinHashEnc → pinUvAuthToken exchange →
pinUvAuthParam, both keying shapes) byte-exact against a live
`python-fido2` capture.**
